vertical scalability= increasing the size of the instance. Also means scaling up or down.
horizontal scalability= increasing the number of instances/ systems for your applications. Used in distributed systems
high availabilty= Runs the same instances of multiple AZ.
passive= means not both are working at the same time
active= means both are defiding the load under them selfs.

load balancing: is forwarding traffic to multiple server downstream. So basically splitsing the load over multiple servers.

elastic load balancer: managed load balacer made by AWS and they also take care of it with the configurations and so on...
  makes use of health checks: checks if there is a response from the device it's trying to reach. Via sending a message on a port.
  4 kinds of load balancers:
    1) classic load balancer/v1/old generation/CLB
      1.1) not used anymore: uses http,https,tcp,ssl
    2) application load balancer/v2: supports http,https,websockets
    3) network load balancer/v2: tcp,tls,udp
    4) gateway load balancer: operates a layer 3 of the osi model. This is the network layer

application load balancer/ALB
-----------------------------
allows for routing tables in different target groups: 
  1) based on path url example.com/users & example.com/posts
  2) based on hostname in url: one.example.com&other.example.com
  3) based on query,string,headers: example.com/users?id=123&order=false
  4) used for micro service&container-based application
  5) has a redirect feature on the port 
target groups:
  1) EC2 instances
  2) ECS tasks
  3) Lambda functions
  4) IP Addresses
servers don't see the IP of the client directly: true IP of the client is inserted in the header of X-Forwarded-For, X-Forwarded-Port and proto/ X-Forwarded-Proto

Network load balancer/NLB
-------------------------------
  1) only one static IP per AZ
  2) ultra-low latency
  3) layer 4 of osi so TCP&UDP traffic
target groups:
  1) EC2 instances
  2) IP addresses= always private IPs
  3) NLB is always in front of ALB
  4) health checks only support TCP,HTTP&HTTPS

GLB/Gateway load balancer
-------------------------
  1) all traffic through firewal
  2) deploy,scale and manage a fleet of 3rd party network virtual applicances in AWS
  3) operates network layer
    3.1)transparant= single entry
    3.2) load balancer
    3.3) geneve = port 608I
  target: 
  1) ec2
  2) IP addresses
users+route table+gateway load balancer+ target group+ application
sticky sessions= same client always redirected to the same instance behind load balancer. Works with a cookie
application-based cookies:
  1) generated by target, includes attributes
  2) generated by the load balancer
duration based cookies: 
  1) generated by load balancer
cross-zone load balancing: traffic is split evenly between different AZ's. With each it's own LB/Load balancer. Enabled by default for application LB for the others you pay.
ACM= AWS certificate manager
X.509 for ssl/TLS certificates
SNI/server name indication= multiple SSL certificates onto one web server, only works for ALB&NLB
connection draining/deregistration delay= time to complete "in-fligh request" while the instance is de-registring or unhealthy so it stops sending new request to the EC2 instances
auto scaling group: scale out/ add ec2 instances, scale in/remove ec2 instances

Minimum capacity → minimum number of instances
Desired capacity → number of instances ASG tries to maintain
Maximum capacity → maximum number of instances
Launch Template → tells the ASG how to launch EC2 instances (AMI, instance type, security groups, etc.)
Scaling policies → determine when to add/remove instances
Health checks → unhealthy instances can be terminated/replaced
Multiple AZs → ASG can distribute instances across AZs for high availability
Dynamic scaling → scale based on metrics such as CPU utilization
Target tracking → e.g. maintain average CPU around 50%
ASG + Load Balancer → common architecture where the LB distributes traffic and the ASG adds/removes EC2 instances
cloudwatch alarms= metric by example average CPU that can use scale out or scale in based on the alarms
difference kind of scaling:
  1) dynamic
    1.1) target tracking scaling
    1.2) simple/step scaling= cloudwatch alarms
  2) sheduled scaling
    2.1) anticipate scaling on patterns
  3) predictive scaling: forecast load and schedule scaling
metric for scaling:
  1) cpu utilization
  2) request count per target
  3) average network in/out
  4) any custom metric
cooling period= not allowed to setup/delete instances

